    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a helpful distinction. I'll definitely look into using the description field for that context. \n\nI'm also a bit worried about the \"unidentified\" part from a long-term preservation standpoint. If we have these maps where the maker is completely obscured or intentionally obscured—almost like they were intentionally being elusive or \"shadowy\"—is there any precedent in archival science for handling \"hidden\" or \"secret\" identities? I don't want to imply something that isn't there, but I also don't want to strip the historical nuance if there's a sense of intentionality behind the anonymity.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Archival science generally advises avoiding assumptions about intent, so avoid labeling obscurity as \"secret\" unless documented by external evidence. Instead, use terminology that reflects observable reality, such as \"Anonymous\" or \"Creator information unavailable,\" within the appropriate metadata fields. If the *act* of anonymity is historically relevant, document that observation thoroughly in the scope and context notes, keeping it separate from the core descriptive fields.",
      "source": "target",
